At the school you said

270
00:19:19.423 --> 00:19:21.093
there were thousands of people
more qualified than you, so

271
00:19:21.117 --> 00:19:23.491
- I was being modest.
- I don't need modest.

272
00:19:23.526 --> 00:19:25.152
I need people
who think they're right

273
00:19:25.187 --> 00:19:26.670
when everyone else
thinks they're wrong.

274
00:19:26.694 --> 00:19:28.166
I need people
who piss other people off.

275
00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:29.299
That's me!

276
00:19:29.334 --> 00:19:31.158
Do you want to be on a project?

277
00:19:31.193 --> 00:19:33.369
If you

278
00:19:33.404 --> 00:19:36.405
If you think I can help, then

279
00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:38.473
Just answer the question.

280
00:19:38.508 --> 00:19:40.409
I wanna help the world.

281
00:19:40.444 --> 00:19:41.971
I left you three dots.

282
00:19:42.006 --> 00:19:43.478
Just three, huh?

283
00:19:43.513 --> 00:19:44.875
Plus the one you killed.

284
00:19:45.878 --> 00:19:47.416
I would take the three.

285
00:19:49.783 --> 00:19:51.354
- Yes?
- I'll take the dots.

286
00:19:51.389 --> 00:19:52.916
Get to work, Dr. Grace.

287
00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:56.953
The world is counting on you.

288
00:21:04.627 --> 00:21:05.956
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

289
00:21:07.157 --> 00:21:08.464
This is Carl.

290
00:21:08.499 --> 00:21:10.257
Imagine for a second

291
00:21:10.292 --> 00:21:12.666
that you're
an interstellar microorganism.

292
00:21:12.701 --> 00:21:14.470
I'm not doing that.

293
00:21:16.232 --> 00:21:19.134
Why would you
leave the sun and go to Venus?

294
00:21:19.169 --> 00:21:21.840
Why not stop
at Mercury? It's closer.

295
00:21:23.349 --> 00:21:25.272
Maybe fresh air?

296
00:21:25.307 --> 00:21:27.208
Venus's atmosphere
is mostly CO2.

297
00:21:27.243 --> 00:21:28.913
Maybe that's fresh air to them.

298
00:21:28.948 --> 00:21:30.868
You don't know what
these little space dots like.

299
00:21:36.087 --> 00:21:37.251
<i>Hey, Carl?</i>

300
00:21:37.286 --> 00:21:39.154
Do we have an expense account?

301
00:21:39.189 --> 00:21:40.925
We don't.

302
00:21:45.195 --> 00:21:46.304
But I do.

303
00:22:17.799 --> 00:22:19.370
All right,
I got something for you.

304
00:22:30.108 --> 00:22:31.613
Thank you.

305
00:22:31.648 --> 00:22:33.252
- Do you have a coupon?
- We don't need a coupon.

306
00:22:33.276 --> 00:22:35.243
- We're the government.
- Which government?

307
00:22:35.278 --> 00:22:36.618
All of 'em.

308
00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.083
So this box is Venus.

309
00:22:40.118 --> 00:22:41.920
The box is Venus.

310
00:22:41.955 --> 00:22:43.955
It's set to emit the same
infrared light frequency

311
00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:45.891
as Venus's CO2 atmosphere.

312
00:22:45.926 --> 00:22:48.124
So if the dots move
when you turn the lights on,

313
00:22:48.159 --> 00:22:50.797
that means
they're looking for CO2.

314
00:22:50.832 --> 00:22:53.294
Thus proving
the Carl Hypothesis.

315
00:22:53.329 --> 00:22:54.361
Sick.

316
00:22:56.838 --> 00:22:58.299
Sorry.

317
00:22:58.334 --> 00:23:00.004
- All right.
- All right.

318
00:23:00.039 --> 00:23:01.676
You ready?

319
00:23:04.516 --> 00:23:06.373
Uh-oh.

320
00:23:06.408 --> 00:23:07.913
Why you saying "Uh-oh"?

321
00:23:07.948 --> 00:23:09.948
The Astrophage
moved off the slide.

322
00:23:09.983 --> 00:23:11.246
Huh?

323
00:23:11.281 --> 00:23:12.885
We lost them.

324
00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:14.854
What do you mean,
we lost them? Go get them.

325
00:23:15.423 --> 00:23:17.219
If I open the door in the light,

326
00:23:17.254 --> 00:23:19.265
they're gonna escape, and then
we'll lose them forever.

327
00:23:19.289 --> 00:23:20.629
At least now they're in the box.

328
00:23:20.664 --> 00:23:22.499
So we'll just
turn the lights off.

329
00:23:22.534 --> 00:23:24.105
But there'll still be light.
It's gotta be total darkness.

330
00:23:24.129 --> 00:23:26.404
Okay, so just put this box
in another box.

331
00:23:35.041 --> 00:23:36.205
Nice.

332
00:23:48.351 --> 00:23:50.593
Go ahead and close it. Over.

333
00:23:51.288 --> 00:23:52.793
I can hear you.

334
00:23:58.334 --> 00:24:00.328
Entering phase two.

335
00:24:05.907 --> 00:24:07.236
I'm in.

336
00:24:12.408 --> 00:24:14.276
<i>Please report. Over.</i>

337
00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:18.885
<i>There's one.</i>

338
00:24:20.449 --> 00:24:21.492
<i>There's two!</i>

339
00:24:21.527 --> 00:24:23.626
Come on! Get the third one.

340
00:24:25.795 --> 00:24:27.421
<i>Got sample three.</i>

341
00:24:27.456 --> 00:24:29.533
<i>The Three Musketeers.
The Three Stooges.</i>

342
00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:34.604
<i>Uh-oh.</i>

343
00:24:34.639 --> 00:24:36.298
What? What?

344
00:24:37.499 --> 00:24:39.235
<i>Are you sitting down?</i>

345
00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:41.072
No, I'm standing
like a grown man.

346
00:24:41.107 --> 00:24:43.173
<i>'Cause there's a fourth.</i>

347
00:24:44.044 --> 00:24:45.076
What?

348
00:24:47.212 --> 00:24:49.443
<i>We're fathers, Carl.</i>

349
00:24:51.821 --> 00:24:53.854
Now I'm sitting down.

350
00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:57.792
<i>Stratt.</i>

351
00:24:57.827 --> 00:25:00.828
Carl and I made a baby.

352
00:25:00.863 --> 00:25:02.258
<i>What?</i>

353
00:25:02.293 --> 00:25:04.194
We figured out
how Astrophage breeds.

354
00:25:05.461 --> 00:25:06.735
<i>Don't move.</i>

355
00:25:08.706 --> 00:25:10.002
Wheels up.

356
00:25:11.401 --> 00:25:12.840
You know, Carl,

357
00:25:12.875 --> 00:25:15.238
when Stratt said
she was sending a jet,

358
00:25:15.273 --> 00:25:16.888
I thought you meant
like a PJ or something.

359
00:25:16.912 --> 00:25:19.616
- I know.
- Take this.

360
00:25:21.081 --> 00:25:22.597
Been a long time
since I've taken a pill

361
00:25:22.621 --> 00:25:24.341
from a stranger
without knowing what it was.

362
00:25:26.449 --> 00:25:28.559
Stop! Stop!

363
00:25:38.967 --> 00:25:40.835
Dr. Grace,
how was your flight?

364
00:25:46.403 --> 00:25:48.073
- Where are we?
- The ocean.

365
00:25:48.108 --> 00:25:51.142
The Astrophage reproduction.
Can you do it at scale?

366
00:25:51.177 --> 00:25:55.047
Oh, in theory, you just get
a bunch of elbow-shaped pipe,

367
00:25:55.082 --> 00:25:56.510
put sunlight in one end...

368
00:25:56.545 --> 00:25:57.753
Excuse me for a second.

369
00:25:59.119 --> 00:26:00.657
- Write it down.
- Yes, ma'am.

370
00:26:02.254 --> 00:26:05.123
And IR light and CO2
in the other. Repeat.

371
00:26:05.158 --> 00:26:06.322
Anything else you need for...

372
00:26:06.357 --> 00:26:07.961
Just some water.

373
00:26:07.996 --> 00:26:09.303
He gave me a pill
and it's still in my throat.

374
00:26:09.327 --> 00:26:10.700
Two venti coffees.

375
00:26:10.735 --> 00:26:12.196
Thank you so much.
Water for him.

376
00:26:12.231 --> 00:26:13.428
Or coffee. That's

377
00:26:13.463 --> 00:26:14.869
I need both.

378
00:26:14.904 --> 00:26:16.332
Or not.

379
00:26:16.367 --> 00:26:17.718
Okay, so this is
kind of a tough room,

380
00:26:17.742 --> 00:26:19.335
so don't take it personally.

381
00:26:19.370 --> 00:26:20.875
I don't need anything fancy.

382
00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:23.174
As long as there's a bed
and a shower I'm good.

383
00:26:28.115 --> 00:26:30.049
- I can't do this.
- Come on. Yes.

384
00:26:30.084 --> 00:26:31.919
- I can't. I can't.
- You're doing great.

385
00:26:31.954 --> 00:26:34.218
Everyone,
this is Dr. Ryland Grace

386
00:26:34.253 --> 00:26:35.582
from the United States.

387
00:26:35.617 --> 00:26:37.892
Please go here. Thank you.

388
00:26:37.927 --> 00:26:40.796
And he figured out
how to breed Astrophage.

389
00:26:46.298 --> 00:26:47.836
How did you do it?

390
00:26:47.871 --> 00:26:49.332
How long does the process take?

391
00:26:49.367 --> 00:26:51.840
Does it reproduce
by mitosis or meiosis?

392
00:26:51.875 --> 00:26:54.172
<i>What is the incubation period?</i>

393
00:26:55.406 --> 00:26:57.043
Um

394
00:26:58.475 --> 00:27:00.981
Carl and I made a mini Venus
out of a plywood box,

395
00:27:01.583 --> 00:27:04.017
and as soon as
the samples recognized

396
00:27:04.052 --> 00:27:07.086
the, uh, spectral signature
of CO2, they were

397
00:27:09.354 --> 00:27:11.222
They were like,
"Whoomp! There it is!"

398
00:27:11.257 --> 00:27:12.388
Okay.

399
00:27:12.423 --> 00:27:13.961
You can sit down.

400
00:27:13.996 --> 00:27:15.523
Who's Carl?

401
00:27:15.558 --> 00:27:17.107
While your experiment
was crude and clumsy,

402
00:27:17.131 --> 00:27:19.593
our scientists
have replicated his results.

403
00:27:22.367 --> 00:27:24.301
I'm sorry, how did you see...?

404
00:27:24.336 --> 00:27:26.171
We estimate
an eight-day doubling time

405
00:27:26.206 --> 00:27:27.502
under optimal conditions.

406
00:27:27.537 --> 00:27:29.812
- Dr. Grace is right.
- Oh.

407
00:27:29.847 --> 00:27:31.308
It is the CO2.

408
00:27:31.343 --> 00:27:32.881
That's why
they're going to Venus.

409
00:27:32.916 --> 00:27:34.179
Please say it for everyone.

410
00:27:34.214 --> 00:27:35.785
That's why
they're going to Venus.

411
00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:38.887
So that's why
they're going to Venus,

412
00:27:38.922 --> 00:27:40.757
is what Dr. Grace wants to say.

413
00:27:40.792 --> 00:27:42.286
They're going to breed.

414
00:27:42.321 --> 00:27:44.222
Whoomp! There it is.

415
00:27:45.797 --> 00:27:47.621
I really like him.

416
00:27:47.656 --> 00:27:50.162
How long
to make two million kilogram?

417
00:27:50.197 --> 00:27:51.702
Two million?

418
00:27:53.563 --> 00:27:54.804
Two million is...

419
00:27:54.839 --> 00:27:55.904
Why

420
00:27:57.699 --> 00:28:00.073
on earth would you need
that much Astrophage?

421
00:28:00.108 --> 00:28:02.207
Nobody told him?

422
00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:04.077
He has no clearance.

423
00:28:04.112 --> 00:28:05.947
Please stand up, Dr. Grace.

424
00:28:06.708 --> 00:28:07.817
Stand up.

425
00:28:08.886 --> 00:28:10.677
I hereby grant you
top-secret clearance

426
00:28:10.712 --> 00:28:13.218
to all information pertaining
to Project Hail Mary.

427
00:28:14.485 --> 00:28:16.089
What's Project Hail Mary?

428
00:28:17.961 --> 00:28:18.993
All right.

429
00:28:22.361 --> 00:28:24.867
The sun
is not the only star dying.

430
00:28:24.902 --> 00:28:27.265
There is a clear pattern
of infection.

431
00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:31.203
Every star was infected
by its neighbor except one.

432
00:28:31.238 --> 00:28:32.633
Tau Ceti?

433
00:28:32.668 --> 00:28:34.371
That's right.
11.9 light years away.

434
00:28:34.406 --> 00:28:36.208
It is uninfected

435
00:28:36.243 --> 00:28:38.221
despite being well within
the cluster of infected stars.

436
00:28:38.245 --> 00:28:39.277
Why?

437
00:28:39.312 --> 00:28:40.410
Why? Tell him.

438
00:28:40.445 --> 00:28:42.115
We don't know!

439
00:28:42.150 --> 00:28:44.414
Which is why
we decided to build a ship

440
00:28:44.449 --> 00:28:46.185
to go there and find out.

441
00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:47.758
It's 11.9 light years away.

442
00:28:47.793 --> 00:28:50.123
You can't just
build an interstellar ship.

443
00:28:50.158 --> 00:28:51.487
Oh yeah, we can.

444
00:28:51.522 --> 00:28:53.159
The ship
is not really the problem.

445
00:28:53.194 --> 00:28:56.591
The energy required to power
the ship is the problem.

446
00:28:56.626 --> 00:28:57.735
It was the problem.

447
00:29:00.234 --> 00:29:01.937
The Astrophage is the fuel.

448
00:29:01.972 --> 00:29:03.730
Provided we can
make enough of it.

449
00:29:03.765 --> 00:29:06.403
And for that,
we need you, my friend.

450
00:29:08.737 --> 00:29:10.308
Me?

451
00:29:10.343 --> 00:29:12.981
Ooh. Those little guys
store a lot of energy.

452
00:29:13.583 --> 00:29:15.181
One mistake
and that much Astrophage

453
00:29:15.216 --> 00:29:17.381
could vaporize California.

454
00:29:17.416 --> 00:29:19.614
That's true, which is why
you now live on a boat

455
00:29:19.649 --> 00:29:21.418
in the middle
of the ocean in case

456
00:29:22.960 --> 00:29:25.092
- I live on a boat?
- You do.

457
00:29:25.127 --> 00:29:27.655
So you wanna build
a near-light speed spaceship,

458
00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:29.998
have it travel further
than any human-made object

459
00:29:30.033 --> 00:29:31.329
has ever traveled,

460
00:29:31.364 --> 00:29:35.102
and visit a star
just to "see what's up"?

461
00:29:35.137 --> 00:29:36.301
Yeah.

462
00:29:36.336 --> 00:29:37.973
And then what?

463
00:29:38.008 --> 00:29:39.810
There won't be
enough fuel for a round-trip

464
00:29:39.845 --> 00:29:43.011
so they send their findings
back to Earth on probes.

465
00:29:45.378 --> 00:29:48.082
And the astronauts

466
00:29:51.285 --> 00:29:52.922
die in space?

467
00:29:52.957 --> 00:29:54.154
Yes.

468
00:29:56.257 --> 00:29:57.861
Space.

469
00:30:01.295 --> 00:30:03.999
Any other plans
you've thought of?

470
00:30:04.034 --> 00:30:06.870
Anything else
you're kicking around?

471
00:30:06.905 --> 00:30:09.169
There are infinite possibilities

472
00:30:09.204 --> 00:30:10.874
for this to go wrong.

473
00:30:10.909 --> 00:30:12.370
It almost certainly won't work.

474
00:30:12.405 --> 00:30:14.768
It's what you Americans
would call a long shot.

475
00:30:15.507 --> 00:30:16.880
Hail Mary.

476
00:30:17.344 --> 00:30:18.673
I get it now.

477
00:30:18.708 --> 00:30:22.314
The alternative
is to just do nothing

478
00:30:23.116 --> 00:30:25.284
and to starve
and to kill each other

479
00:30:25.319 --> 00:30:27.715
and watch everything
on this planet go extinct,

480
00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:29.024
including us.

481
00:30:29.653 --> 00:30:30.751
So

482
00:30:51.180 --> 00:30:53.708
That's a long time
to be in space.

483
00:30:53.743 --> 00:30:55.413
They'll be in comas
for most of it.

484
00:30:55.948 --> 00:30:57.712
But is that even safe?

485
00:30:57.747 --> 00:30:59.747
Nothing about this is safe.

486
00:31:14.632 --> 00:31:15.972
<i>We only need three.</i>

487
00:31:16.898 --> 00:31:18.073
<i>A pilot.</i>

488
00:31:18.108 --> 00:31:20.174
<i>An engineer.</i>

489
00:31:20.209 --> 00:31:22.407
<i>And a scientist.</i>

490
00:31:27.216 --> 00:31:28.776
Commander Yáo.

491
00:31:31.121 --> 00:31:33.649
I must have known you,
but I just don't remember.

492
00:31:34.619 --> 00:31:37.158
You make a funny face

493
00:31:37.193 --> 00:31:40.821
in literally every picture.

494
00:31:41.857 --> 00:31:44.792
<i>You must have been very smart</i>

495
00:31:44.827 --> 00:31:45.859
<i>and strong</i>

496
00:31:47.698 --> 00:31:49.071
and brave.

497
00:31:53.176 --> 00:31:54.208
Ilyukhina.

498
00:31:55.706 --> 00:31:58.179
First, I owe you
three bags of vodka.

499
00:31:59.314 --> 00:32:01.545
<i>You seemed
to have had a lot of friends.</i>

500
00:32:02.218 --> 00:32:03.613
<i>This picture of you,</i>

501
00:32:03.648 --> 00:32:06.957
what looks like
sneaking into the Kremlin

502
00:32:08.686 --> 00:32:09.751
is legendary.

503
00:32:12.624 --> 00:32:14.459
<i>I wish you were still here.</i>

504
00:32:15.858 --> 00:32:18.100
<i>I wish I wasn't alone.</i>

505
00:32:18.135 --> 00:32:19.893
I wish I was

506
00:32:21.006 --> 00:32:23.237
doing a better job, I just, uh

507
00:32:28.112 --> 00:32:30.013
You were both very loved.

508
00:32:31.940 --> 00:32:33.220
You deserve much more than this.

509
00:32:35.911 --> 00:32:37.449
I'll do my best

510
00:32:37.484 --> 00:32:39.055
to make sure that you don't

511
00:32:41.785 --> 00:32:43.785
you know, that you didn't...

512
00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:57.337
I'll do my best.

513
00:34:02.800 --> 00:34:04.998
<i>Approaching Tau Ceti orbit.</i>

514
00:34:05.033 --> 00:34:06.373
<i>Prepare for engine cutoff.</i>

515
00:34:08.003 --> 00:34:09.541
<i>-Fourteen, thirteen</i>
-What?

516
00:34:09.576 --> 00:34:11.081
Why engine cutoff?

517
00:34:11.116 --> 00:34:13.743
Seems like something
we should talk about.

518
00:34:13.778 --> 00:34:15.514
<i>Pilot detected.</i>

519
00:34:15.549 --> 00:34:17.714
What? No. I'm not a pilot!

520
00:34:17.749 --> 00:34:19.221
<i>Four, three</i>

521
00:34:19.256 --> 00:34:21.190
What happens at zero? I mean...

522
00:34:29.057 --> 00:34:31.464
<i>You are now orbiting Tau Ceti.</i>

523
00:34:34.403 --> 00:34:38.174
What the fudging fudger?

524
00:34:40.904 --> 00:34:43.212
<i>Petrovascope operational.</i>

525
00:34:43.247 --> 00:34:45.280
<i>Petrovascope operational.</i>

526
00:35:06.468 --> 00:35:07.566
It's Tau Ceti.

527
00:35:18.546 --> 00:35:21.008
You have a Petrova line.

528
00:35:21.043 --> 00:35:22.911
But you're not dimming.

529
00:35:22.946 --> 00:35:24.880
Why?

530
00:35:34.859 --> 00:35:36.298
What is that?

531
00:35:53.878 --> 00:35:55.647
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

532
00:35:56.584 --> 00:35:58.045
What's a Blip-A?

533
00:35:58.916 --> 00:36:01.158
<i>Blip-A detected.</i>

534
00:37:06.049 --> 00:37:09.424
No, no. No, no, no.
Let's go. Let's go, Mary.

